.TH PROTLOCUS 1 "3 November 2026" "ProtLocus 0.1.0" "Bioinformatics tools"
.SH NAME
.PP
protlocus - high-recall protein/profile-to-genome alignment with splicing and frameshifts
.SH SYNOPSIS
* Indexing a genome (recommended as indexing can be slow and memory hungry):
.RS 4
protlocus
.RB [ -t
.IR nThreads ]
.B -d
.I ref.mpi
.I ref.fna
.RE

* Aligning proteins to a genome:
.RS 4
protlocus 
.RB [ -t
.IR nThreads ]
.I ref.mpi
.I protein.faa
>
.I output.paf
.br
protlocus 
.RB [ -t
.IR nThreads ]
.I ref.fna
.I protein.faa
>
.I output.paf
.RE

* Aligning HMMER3 profiles (one or a whole library) to a genome:
.RS 4
protlocus
.RB [ -t
.IR nThreads ]
.RB [ --gpu ]
.I ref.mpi
.I library.hmm
>
.I output.paf
.RE

* Scanning a whole HMM library with the built-in prescreen (recommended):
.RS 4
protlocus
.RB [ -t
.IR nThreads ]
.B --accuracy --prescreen
.RB [ --tier=fast|balanced|sensitive ]
.I ref.mpi
.I library.hmm
>
.I output.paf
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
ProtLocus aligns protein sequences or HMMER3 profiles to a genome allowing
potential frameshifts and splicing. It is a derivative of miniprot: without
.BR --accuracy ,
.B --gpu
or a
.I .hmm
query its output is intended to match upstream miniprot 0.18. See HMMER3
PROFILE QUERIES below for the profile path.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Indexing options
.TP 10
.BI -k \ INT
K-mer size for genome-wide indexing [6]
.TP
.BI -M \ INT
Sample k-mers at a rate
.RI 1/2** INT
[1]. Increasing this option reduces peak memory but decreases sensitivity.
.TP
.BI -L \ INT
Minimum ORF length to index [30]
.TP
.BI -T \ INT
NCBI translation table (1 through 33 except 7-8 and 17-20) [1]
.TP
.BI -b \ INT
Number of bits per bin [8]. ProtLocus splits the genome into non-overlapping bins of 2^8 bp in size.
.TP
.BI -d \ FILE
Write the index to
.I FILE
[].
.SS Chaining options
.TP 10
.B -S
Disable splicing. It applies
.RB ` -G1k
.B -J1k
.BR -e1k '
at the same time.
.TP
.BI -c \ NUM
Ignore k-mers occurring
.I NUM
times or more [50k]
.TP
.BI -G \ NUM
Max intron size [200k]. This option overrides
.BR -I .
.TP
.BI -I
Set max intron size to
.RI min(max(3.6*sqrt( refLen ),10000),300000)
where
.I refLen
is the total length of the input genome.
.TP
.BI -n \ NUM
Min number of syncmers in a chain [10]
.TP
.BI -m \ NUM
Min chaining score [0]
.TP
.BI -l \ INT
K-mer size for the second round of chaining [5]
.TP
.BI -e \ NUM
Max extension from chain ends for alignment or the second round of chaining [10k]
.TP
.BI -p \ FLOAT
Filter out a secondary chain/alignment if its score is
.I FLOAT
fraction of the best chain [0.5]
.TP
.BI -N \ NUM
Retain at most
.I NUM
number of secondary chains/alignments [30]
.SS Alignment options
.TP 10
.BI -O \ INT
Gap open penalty [11]
.TP
.BI -E \ INT
Gap extension penalty [1]. A gap of size
.B g
costs 
.RB { -O }+{ -E }* g .
.TP
.BI -J \ INT
Intron open penalty [29]
.TP
.BI -F \ INT
Penalty for frameshifts or in-frame stop codons [23]
.TP
.BI -C \ FLOAT
Weight of splicing penalty [1]. Set to 0 to ignore splicing signals.
.TP
.BI -B \ IN
Bonus score for alignment reaching ends of proteins [5]
.TP
.BI -j \ INT
Splice model for the target genome: 2=vertebrate/insect, 1=general, 0=none [1]. The vertebrate/insect
model considers `G|GTR...YYYNYAG|' as the optimal splicing sequence and
penalizes other sequences based on profiles in Sibley et al (2016). According
to Irimia and Roy (2008) and Sheth et al (2006), the first `G' in the donor
exon and the poly-Y close to the acceptor may not be conserved in some species.
The general model takes `|GTR...YAG|' as the optimal sequence. Both models also
slightly prefer less frequent splice sites including `G|GC...YAG|' and `|AT...AC|'.
.TP
.BI --spsc \ FILE
Splice score file []. Each line is TAB-delimited, consisting of contig name,
offset of the splice junction, strand (`+' or `-'), donor or acceptor (`D' or
`A') and an integer score. The score is added the donor/acceptor score function.
It can be positive or negative and needs to be compatible with the scoring system.
This option additionally increases
.B -J
and
.B --J2
by 10 unless they are specified on the command line.
.TP
.BI --spsc0 \ INT
Splice score for positions not in the
.B --spsc
file [-7]. This option has no effect if
.B --spsc
is not specified.
.TP
.BI --spsc-max \ INT
Cap splice scores to
.I INT
[14].
.TP
.BI --io-coef \ FLOAT
Logarithm intron length penalty (EXPERIMENTAL) [0.5]
.SS Accuracy options
.TP 10
.B --accuracy
Enable the high-recall mode: repetitive-seed rescue, per-contig/strand
chaining, relaxed candidate retention, no-trusted-anchor DP rescue,
suspicious-junction re-refinement and composite ranking. Slower than the
default mode. Every mechanism has an
.B --acc-no-*
switch and every threshold an
.B --acc-*
knob; see
.B protlocus --help
and ACCURACY.md.
.TP
.B --fast
Speed preset of
.BR --accuracy :
implies
.B --accuracy --acc-end-dyn --acc-end-aa-cap
(end-extension windows scaled with the query length). About 20-25% less
alignment time for a small recall cost.
.TP
.B --profile
Write per-stage timing and workload counters to stderr as one
.B [M::profile]
line at the end of the run.
.TP
.B --compact
Identification preset for compact, gene-dense, intron-poor genomes such as
yeast. Implies
.BR --accuracy ,
raises the anchor-rescue budgets, enables
.B --acc-strict-multi-exon
and
.B --acc-strict-span-ratio=4
to reject hits spilling across neighbouring genes, sets
.B --acc-min-score=15 --acc-min-pos-ratio=0.50
and scales the maximum intron length with the genome size as
.BR -I .
.SS Candidate-locus and prescreen options (HMM library scans)
.TP 10
.B --prescreen
Build a six-frame ORF spaced-k-mer index of the genome in-process and generate
each profile's candidate loci from it (MMseqs2-style profile k-mer prefilter,
ungapped diagonal, banded gapped alignment, Karlin-Altschul E-value). Loci
with E-value at or below the tier cutoff replace the genome-wide seed/chain
stage exactly like a
.BR --cand-table .
Mutually exclusive with
.BR --cand-table .
Defaults to
.B --tier=balanced
when neither
.B --tier
nor
.B --cand-max-evalue
is given.
.TP
.BI --tier= STR
Sensitivity tier = E-value cutoff on the candidate loci:
.BR fast ,
.B balanced
or
.BR sensitive .
With
.B --prescreen
(built-in E-values) the cutoffs are E<=0.1/100/10000; with
.B --cand-table
(mmseqs E-values) they are E<=100/1000/10000.
.B sensitive
also lowers the prescreen k-mer threshold by one.
.TP
.BI --small-genome= STR
.BR auto / on / off
[auto]. With
.BR --prescreen ,
when the genome total length is at or below
.B --small-genome-size
(or
.B on
forces it), switch to the small-genome recall preset
.B --ps-kmer=5 --acc-min-ps=0 --acc-min-score=0 --acc-min-pos-ratio=0
so weak hits reach the output; filter them with
.B protlocus-recheck
(E<=1e-5). Any of these four flags given explicitly on the command line is
kept. On E. coli and yeast this recovers 4-5 percentage points of domain
recall versus the defaults at unchanged purity.
.TP
.BI --small-genome-size= NUM
Genome-size threshold that triggers
.B --small-genome=auto
[50M].
.TP
.BI --cand-max-evalue= FLOAT
Arbitrary E-value cutoff on the candidate loci (ignored when
.B --tier
is given); <=0 keeps every listed locus [0].
.TP
.BI --cand-table= FILE
External candidate loci, TSV
.IR "query name, chrom, start0, end0, strand[, evalue]"
(for example from
.BR tools/mmseqs2cand.py ).
Only the listed loci are refined and aligned; queries absent from the table
get no hit.
.TP
.BI --ps-sens= FLOAT
Prefilter sensitivity on the mmseqs
.B -s
scale; sets the profile k-mer score threshold [7.5].
.TP
.BI --ps-kmer= INT
Spaced k-mer size, 5..7 (index has 20^k buckets: k=6 about 0.5 GB, k=7 about
10 GB) [6].
.TP
.BI --ps-kmer-thr= NUM
Explicit k-mer score threshold in half-bits, overriding
.BR --ps-sens ;
<=0 derives it from
.B --ps-sens
(k=6: 22) [0].
.TP
.BI --ps-kmer-max= NUM
Cap on similar k-mers generated per profile window.
.TP
.BI --ps-min-orf= INT
Ignore stop-free ORFs shorter than INT aa [30].
.TP
.BI --ps-min-ungapped= NUM
Ungapped diagonal score floor in half-bits [15].
.TP
.BI --ps-max-seqs= NUM
ORFs per profile passed to the gapped stage [4000].
.TP
.BI --ps-band= INT
Gapped alignment band (aa) around the best ungapped diagonal; <=0 = full
matrix [32].
.TP
.BI --ps-dump= FILE
Also write the generated candidate table (same TSV as
.BR --cand-table ,
6th column = E-value).
.TP
.B --no-cand-diag
Do not seed a prescreen locus from its best ungapped PSSM diagonal when the
5-mer refine chain is empty (ablation switch; loses divergent family members).
.TP
.BI --acc-min-ps= NUM
Drop a profile-query hit whose HMM Viterbi score (PAF
.BR ps )
is below NUM, on top of the DP-score floor; <=0 disables; no effect on
protein queries [60].
.TP
.BI --acc-ps-rescue= AS[,POSR]
Exempt a hit from
.B --acc-min-ps
when its DP score is at least AS and its positive ratio at least POSR (short
models may fail ps>=60 with strong alignments); AS<=0 disables.
.TP
.BI --thr-table= FILE
Per-query DP-score floor (TSV
.IR "query name, min_AS" ),
e.g. decoy-calibrated per-family thresholds; replaces
.B --acc-min-score
for listed queries. Kept for reproducibility; the global floors are the
recommended protocol.
.TP
.BI --thr-fallback= NUM
Floor for queries absent from
.BR --thr-table ;
<=0 keeps
.B --acc-min-score
[0].
.TP
.B --thr-use-ps
Apply the
.B --thr-table
floor to
.B ps
instead of
.BR AS .
Prefer
.BR --acc-min-ps .
.SS GPU options
.TP 10
.B --gpu
Use the OpenCL device for the terminal-extension DP (all extension requests of
a query are pooled and executed by a persistent multi-queue executor), for
large inter-anchor gap DP (see
.BR --gpu-min-cells )
and, with the environment variable
.BR MP_HMM_GPU=1 ,
for HMM profile scoring. Requires a build with
.BR "make gpu=1" .
Anything the device cannot take falls back to the CPU SIMD kernels. Extension
and HMM scores equal the CPU kernels bit-for-bit; the CIGAR-producing gap DP
returns the same score but may pick a different equal-score path (see
LIMITATIONS). If no OpenCL device is available a warning
is printed and the run continues on the CPU.
.TP
.BI --gpu-device= INT
Zero-based OpenCL GPU index [0].
.TP
.BI --gpu-min-cells= NUM
Minimum number of DP cells (nt length times aa length) for an inter-anchor gap
DP to be submitted to the GPU [1M]. Does not apply to the extension pipeline,
which takes every request.
.TP
.B --gpu-prefilter
After coarse chaining, score each candidate chain's anchors with a batched
GPU gapless X-drop kernel and keep only the top
.B --gpu-prefilter-topk
chains by gapless score (with a per-contig/strand diversity floor, see
.BR --gpu-prefilter-diversity ).
Requires a build with
.BR "make cuda=1" ;
on a CPU-only or
.B "make gpu=1"
(OpenCL) build the flag is accepted but disabled with a warning and mapping
proceeds unfiltered.
.TP
.BI --gpu-prefilter-topk= NUM
Number of chains kept per query by
.B --gpu-prefilter
[32].
.TP
.BI --gpu-prefilter-diversity= NUM
Minimum surviving chains per distinct target/strand group [1].
.TP
.BI --gpu-prefilter-xdrop= NUM
X-drop threshold for the GPU gapless extension used by
.B --gpu-prefilter
[40].
.TP
.B --gpu-prefilter-adaptive
Instead of the fixed
.BR --gpu-prefilter-topk ,
pick K per query from {1,2,4,8,
.BR --gpu-prefilter-topk }
based on the score margin between the best and runner-up chain. Implies
.BR --gpu-prefilter .
.SS Input/Output options
.TP 10
.BI -t \ INT
Number of threads [4]
.TP
.I query.hmm
A query file whose name ends in
.I .hmm
is read as HMMER3 text profiles instead of protein FASTA. See HMMER3 PROFILE
QUERIES below.
.TP
.B --gff
Output in the GFF3 format. `##PAF' lines in the output provide detailed
alignments.
.TP
.B --gff-only
Output in the GFF3 format without `##PAF' lines.
.TP
.B --aln
Output the residue alignment in three lines, where line `##ATN' for the target
nucleotide sequence, `##ATA' for translated amino acid sequence and `##AQA' for
the query protein sequence. On a `##ATA' line, `!' denotes a frameshift
insertion corresponding to the `F' CIGAR operator and `$' denotes a frameshift
substitution corresponding to the `G' operator.
.TP
.B --trans
Output translated protein sequences on `##STA' lines.
.TP
.B --no-cs
Do not output the cs tag
.TP
.BI --max-intron-out \ NUM
In the
.B --aln
format, if an intron is longer than
.IR NUM ,
only output
.RI ceil( NUM /2)
basepairs at the donor or the acceptor sites and write the full intron length
.I LEN
as
.RI ~ LEN ~
in the middle [200].
.TP
.BI -P \ STR
Prefix for IDs in GFF3 or GTF [MP].
.B --gff-delim
overrides this option.
.TP
.BI --gff-delim \ CHAR
Change the ID field in GFF3 to
.RI QueryName CHAR HitIndex
[]. If not specified, the default ID looks like `MP000012'. This option is only
applicable to the GFF3 output format.
.TP
.B --gtf
Output in the GTF format
.TP
.B -u
Print unmapped query proteins
.TP
.BI --outn \ NUM
Output up to
.RI min{ NUM ,
.BR -N }
alignments per query [1000].
.TP
.BI --outs \ FLOAT
Output an alignment only if its score is at least
.IR FLOAT *bestScore,
where bestScore is the best alignment score of the protein [0.99]
.TP
.BI --outc \ FLOAT
Output an alignment only if
.I FLOAT
fraction of the query protein is aligned [0.1]
.TP
.BI -K \ NUM
Query batch size [2M]
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
.SS The GFF3 Format
ProtLocus outputs alignment in the extended Pairwise mApping Format (PAF) by
default (see the next subsection). It can also output GFF3 with option
.BR --gff .
ProtLocus may output three features: `mRNA', `CDS' or `stop_codon'. Here, a
stop_codon is only reported if the alignment reaches the C-terminus of the
protein and the next codon is a stop codon. Per GenCode rule, stop_codon is not
part of CDS but it is part of mRNA or exon.

ProtLocus may output the following attributes in GFF3:
.TS
center box;
cb | cb | cb
l | c | l .
Attribute	Type	Description
_
ID	str	mRNA identifier
Parent	str	Identifier of the parent feature
Rank	int	Rank among all hits of the query
Identity	real	Fraction of exact amino acid matches
Positive	real	Fraction of positive amino acid matches
Donor	str	2bp at the donor site if not GT
Acceptor	str	2bp at the acceptor site if not AG
Frameshift	int	Number of frameshift events in alignment
StopCodon	int	Number of in-frame stop codons
Target	str	Protein coordinate in alignment
.TE

.SS The PAF Format
PAF gives detailed alignment. It is a TAB-delimited text format with each line
consisting of at least 12 fields as are described in the following table:
.TS
center box;
cb | cb | cb
r | c | l .
Col	Type	Description
_
1	string	Protein sequence name
2	int	Protein sequence length
3	int	Protein start coordinate (0-based)
4	int	Protein end coordinate (0-based)
5	char	`+' for forward strand; `-' for reverse
6	string	Contig sequence name
7	int	Contig sequence length
8	int	Contig start coordinate on the original strand
9	int	Contig end coordinate on the original strand
10	int	Number of matching nucleotides
11	int	Number of nucleotides in alignment excl. introns
12	int	Mapping quality (0-255 with 255 for missing)
.TE

.PP
PAF may optionally have additional fields in the SAM-like typed key-value
format. ProtLocus may output the following tags:
.TS
center box;
cb | cb | cb
r | c | l .
Tag	Type	Description
_
AS	i	Alignment score from dynamic programming
ms	i	Alignment score excluding introns
np	i	Number of amino acid matches with positive scores
fs	i	Number of frameshifts
st	i	Number of in-frame stop codons
da	i	Distance to the nearest start codon
do	i	Distance to the nearest stop codon
cg	Z	Protein CIGAR
cs	Z	Difference string
.TE

.PP
A protein CIGAR consists of the following operators:
.TS
center box;
cb | cb
r | l .
Op	Description
_
nM	Alignment match. Consuming n*3 nucleotides and n amino acids
nI	Insertion. Consuming n amino acids
nD	Deletion. Consuming n*3 nucleotides
nF	Frameshift deletion. Consuming n nucleotides
nG	Frameshift match. Consuming n nucleotides and 1 amino acid
nN	Phase-0 intron. Consuming n nucleotides
nU	Phase-1 intron. Consuming n nucleotides and 1 amino acid
nV	Phase-2 intron. Consuming n nucleotides and 1 amino acid
.TE

.PP
The
.B cs
tag encodes difference sequences. It consists of a series of operations:
.TS
center box;
cb | cb |cb
r | l | l .
Op	Regex	Description
_
 :	[0-9]+	Number of identical amino acids
 *	[acgtn]+[A-Z*]	Substitution: ref to query
 +	[A-Z]+	# aa inserted to the reference
 -	[acgtn]+	# nt deleted from the reference
 ~	[acgtn]{2}[0-9]+[acgtn]{2}	Intron length and splice signal
.TE

.SH HMMER3 PROFILE QUERIES
A query file ending in
.I .hmm
may hold one or many HMMER3 profiles (for example
.BR "cat *.hmm > library.hmm" ).
Each profile needs
.BR NAME ,
.BR LENG ,
.BR "ALPH amino" ,
the
.B HMM
header line, the per-position match-emission and transition lines and the
.B //
terminator;
.B COMPO
is skipped. Every profile is mapped as an independent query in the same
multi-threaded mini-batch pipeline as FASTA input, and
.I .hmm
and FASTA files may be mixed on one command line.
.PP
Seeds are taken from the profile consensus (highest-emission residue per
position) and from up to 64 variant sequences that substitute near-tie
residues, so ambiguous columns do not lose seeds. Candidate regions are scored
by a position-specific Viterbi DP over match/insert/delete states; all regions
of a query are scored in one batched pass. In PAF the query name is
.BR NAME ,
the query length is
.B LENG
and
.B AS:i
is the Viterbi score. Residue alignment, CIGAR, GFF3/GTF,
.BR --aln ,
.B --trans
and
.B -u
use the consensus sequence and work unchanged.
.PP
For whole-library scans (thousands of profiles) use
.B --prescreen
with a
.BR --tier :
candidate loci are then generated in-process from a six-frame ORF index instead
of genome-wide k-mer seeding, which is both faster and more sensitive for
divergent families. Single or few profiles work well with the default
seeding path (optionally with
.BR --gpu ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP 10
.B MP_HMM_GPU
If set, HMM profile scoring runs on the OpenCL device (one work-group per
candidate region) for profiles with at most 1023 match states; longer profiles
use the CPU. Off by default because it competes with the extension pipeline
for the device.
.TP
.B MP_HMM_GPU_DEBUG
Print HMM GPU batch statistics at exit.
.TP
.B NS_EXT_GPU_QUEUES
Number of OpenCL command queues used by the extension executor [8, max 16].
.TP
.B NS_EXT_GPU_DEBUG
1: print one
.B [ns_ext_gpu]
statistics line at exit (flushes, launches, work-groups, GPU/CPU request
counts, upload volume, cells, prep/upload/device/cpu/wait seconds); 2: also
log every launch; 3: also verify the device-side input preparation against the
host.
.TP
.B NS_EXT_GPU_NO_WARP
Do not build the warp-synchronous (inline PTX) kernel; use the portable
work-group kernel.
.TP
.B NS_EXT_GPU_STRICT
Abort instead of silently falling back to the CPU when a GPU-eligible
extension request cannot be served by the device.
.TP
.B NS_GPU_DEBUG_CHECK
Verify the nt/aa span of every sub-alignment CIGAR and dump the inputs and
scoring options on mismatch.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.TP 2
*
The initial conditions of dynamic programming are not technically correct,
which may result in suboptimal residue alignment in rare cases.
.TP
*
Support for non-splicing alignment needs to be improved.
.TP
*
With
.BR --gpu ,
about 0.7% of PAF lines differ from the CPU build: almost all describe the
same locus with a different equal-score alignment path (CIGAR, cs, ms, np,
columns 10-11), a few have shifted boundaries. The extension DP itself is
bit-identical; the differences come from tie-breaking in the CIGAR-producing
gap DP. GPU reruns are byte-identical.
.TP
*
HMM profile queries seed from the consensus; a family with no recognisable
consensus k-mers in the genome can be missed even if the profile would score
it.
